package cosmonic:agent@0.4.4;

/// The types that describe where a session stands, with no functions, so an
/// interface that carries a status (a child's state, say) can use them
/// without importing `session-status` itself.
///
/// A status is three facts that hold independently, so every combination is
/// valid and none hides another.
///
/// - `activity`: what is in progress now.
/// - `needs-input`: whether something is held for a person.
/// - `last-run`: how the most recent run that ended did so.
///
/// The implementer, whatever serves the import that returns a status, sets
/// all three only from what it observed itself: the run or compaction it is
/// driving, what it holds at its gates, and how each run it drove ended. That
/// is the outcome or error `run` returned, a cancellation it carried out, a
/// trap, or a stop or restart that cut the run off. Entries an agent writes
/// to the journal, `agent.run-result.v1` among them, are the agent's own
/// report and never set any of the three, and nothing a model says does
/// either. The one agent-reported value is the `finish` inside
/// `run-end.replied`, which arrived in the run's outcome: a caller may route
/// on it and must never allow anything on it. A run that finishes
/// `input-required` does not set `needs-input`; the person's answer starts
/// the next run.
///
/// One state to show. A caller that wants a single value for a badge or a
/// list takes the first that applies:
///
/// 1. needs input, when `needs-input` is set;
/// 2. working, when `activity` is not `idle`, which includes an activity
///    this version does not name: something is in progress;
/// 3. failed, when `last-run` ended `failed` or `interrupted`;
/// 4. done, when `last-run` ended `replied`;
/// 5. idle, otherwise, including after a cancelled run or an ending this
///    version does not name.
///
/// A value it does not know on one fact leaves the other two readable.
///
/// Adding a case to a variant here is a breaking change. Until a release
/// adds one, the implementer reports an activity, a hold or a run ending this
/// version does not name as `other`, carrying the kebab-case name the case
/// would have. That name is never empty; a caller may show it and acts on
/// nothing. Error detail is different: `status-error` carries optional
/// diagnostic text that a caller may log and never parses.
///
/// Every string the implementer puts in a status or an error is its own
/// words, at most 256 bytes: never text an agent, a model or a tool wrote,
/// never a secret or credential value, and never a binding, storage or
/// infrastructure name the caller was not shown. The one exception is the
/// agent's `finish`, which is marked as the agent's own wherever it appears.
///
/// Times are `wasi:clocks/system-clock` instants, so a component that uses
/// these types also imports `system-clock`.
///
/// Unstable behind `agent-session-status`, which needs `agent-run` as well,
/// for `finish`. The shape may change before it is marked `@since`.
@unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
interface session-status-types {
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    use run-types.{finish};
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    use wasi:clocks/system-clock@0.3.0.{instant};

    /// What is in progress in the session now. Says nothing about whether a
    /// person is needed or how earlier runs ended.
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    variant activity {
        /// Nothing is in progress: no run and no compaction.
        idle,
        /// A run is in progress, with the `run-id` its driver gave it, or
        /// `none` when the implementer withholds the id from this caller.
        /// It starts when the implementer accepts the run, so a `run` call
        /// refused with a `start-error` never shows here. A run that waits
        /// on a person is still `running`, and so is a cancelled run that
        /// has not yet stopped, as `run` stays `busy` until it has.
        running(option<string>),
        /// A compaction the driver asked for through `compact` is in
        /// progress. One the agent starts during a run is part of `running`.
        compacting,
        /// An activity this version does not name, by its kebab-case name,
        /// which is never empty. Something is in progress.
        other(string),
    }

    /// What kind of thing is held for a person.
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    variant hold-kind {
        /// A tool call or outbound request waiting to be allowed.
        approval,
        /// A tool waiting to be added to the session.
        tool-addition,
        /// A change waiting for review.
        review,
        /// A kind this version does not name, by its kebab-case name, which
        /// is never empty. Something is still held for a person.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Something the implementer holds at one of its gates for a person.
    /// When it holds several, this describes the oldest.
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    record hold {
        /// What kind of thing is held.
        kind: hold-kind,
        /// When it was raised. `none` when the implementer does not know.
        since: option<instant>,
    }

    /// How a run ended, as the implementer observed it. A `run` call refused
    /// with a `start-error` was never accepted, so it never ends here and
    /// leaves `last-run` as it was.
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    variant run-end {
        /// `run` returned a `run-outcome`, carrying the finish the agent
        /// reported. Agent-reported: a caller routes on it and never allows
        /// anything on it, and treats the text in `finish.other` as the
        /// agent's own. A run whose outcome `run` had already returned when
        /// a cancellation arrived ends here, not `cancelled`.
        replied(finish),
        /// The run failed: `run` returned a `run-error` other than
        /// `cancelled`, or the agent trapped while running it.
        failed,
        /// Cut off because the host stopped or restarted before the run
        /// recorded a result. A run whose own result is in the journal ended
        /// as that result says; a closing entry the host wrote for it
        /// afterwards does not change this.
        interrupted,
        /// The implementer carried out a cancellation before `run` returned
        /// an outcome: `run` returned `run-error.cancelled`, or the
        /// implementer stopped the run by force to cancel it.
        cancelled,
        /// An ending this version does not name, by its kebab-case name,
        /// which is never empty. Show it as the run having ended; act on
        /// nothing.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A run that has ended.
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    record ended-run {
        /// The `run-id` its driver gave it. `none` when the implementer
        /// withholds the id from this caller.
        run: option<string>,
        /// How it ended.
        end: run-end,
        /// When it ended. `none` when the implementer does not know.
        ended-at: option<instant>,
    }

    /// A session's status: three independent facts.
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    record session-status {
        /// What is in progress now.
        activity: activity,
        /// When `activity` took its current value. `none` when the
        /// implementer does not know, such as a session that has never run.
        activity-since: option<instant>,
        /// Set while the host holds something for a person, whether or not
        /// a run is in progress. Time held during a run counts toward that
        /// run's `run-options.max-duration`.
        /// For a spawned child whose run waits on a tool call or load held
        /// this way, its caller sees `run-progress.blocked(tool-approval)`:
        /// the same condition, seen at run level.
        needs-input: option<hold>,
        /// The most recent run that ended. Stays set while a later run is
        /// in progress, and changes only when a run ends or when the driver
        /// or a person clears the session, which resets it to `none`. A
        /// clear the agent makes through its own `session` import leaves it
        /// as it was, so an agent cannot hide how its last run ended. `none`
        /// when no run has ended since the session began or was last cleared
        /// that way.
        last-run: option<ended-run>,
    }

    /// Why the status could not be read, or why a watch stopped.
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    variant status-error {
        /// This caller may not read the session's status, or no longer may.
        denied(option<string>),
        /// The session has ended: it was deleted or its workload was removed.
        /// Nothing more will be reported, so calling again will not help.
        ended,
        /// The implementer could not serve the call, or could not keep a
        /// watch going. Trying again later may succeed. The detail is for
        /// logs.
        unavailable(option<string>),
        /// A failure not captured above, with optional diagnostic detail.
        /// Never parse it.
        other(option<string>),
    }
}

/// Read and follow where a session stands. The types, and the rules for how
/// the implementer sets them, are in `session-status-types`.
///
/// As with `session` and `run`, the implementer binds the session and the
/// caller never names one. The import suits a component that drives or
/// watches an agent, such as an adapter serving a status endpoint, bound to
/// that agent's session. Reading the status never grants driving the session.
///
/// Unstable behind `agent-session-status`, which needs `agent-run` as well.
/// The shape may change before it is marked `@since`.
@unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
interface session-status {
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    use session-status-types.{session-status, status-error};

    /// The session's status now. Once the session has ended, `ended`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    get: async func() -> result<session-status, status-error>;

    /// Follow the session's status.
    ///
    /// The stream's first item is the status when the watch began, then each
    /// change to any of its facts, in order. A slow reader may miss a
    /// short-lived value but never sees an older status after a newer one.
    /// Clearing the session is a change like any other, not an end: the
    /// stream goes on with `last-run` reset to `none`.
    ///
    /// The future says why the stream ended, and resolves only once it has.
    /// `ok` means the caller dropped the stream. An error carries what a
    /// fresh `get` would answer at that moment: `ended` when the session
    /// ended, `denied` when this caller's access to the status was
    /// withdrawn, which ends every watch it holds, or `unavailable` when the
    /// implementer could not keep the watch going. After `unavailable` the
    /// caller may call `watch` again; the new stream's first item is the
    /// status then, so no change is lost beyond the short-lived values a
    /// slow reader may miss anyway.
    ///
    /// An implementer bounds how many watches a caller holds open at once.
    /// Past that bound, `watch` returns `unavailable` without starting one.
    /// The outer error is for a watch that never started, for the same
    /// reasons `get` gives.
    @unstable(feature = agent-session-status)
    watch: async func() -> result<tuple<stream<session-status>, future<result<_, status-error>>>, status-error>;
}
